window.PORTAL={"day": 11, "title": "ROP + IVH + PVL + Neurodevelopmental Follow-up", "docTitle": "DAY 11 — RETINOPATHY OF PREMATURITY, IVH, PVL & NEURODEVELOPMENTAL FOLLOW-UP", "subtitle": ["NEET-SS + ICP PAEDIATRICS", "Friday, 9 October 2026  •  FINAL V7  •  Nelson 22e primary conceptual reference  •  Cloherty & Stark 9e neonatal management cross-reference  •  IAP/IAP-NNF + AIIMS Indian clinical overlay  •  Current authoritative international protocols"], "source": "source/Day11_NEETSS_ICP_FINAL_V7_Nelson_Cloherty_IAP_AIIMS.docx", "pages": [{"id": "overview", "title": "Overview", "group": "Start", "kind": "overview", "sections": [{"title": "", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "NEET-SS + ICP PAEDIATRICS"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Friday, 9 October 2026  •  FINAL V7  •  Nelson 22e primary conceptual reference  •  Cloherty &amp; Stark 9e neonatal management cross-reference  •  IAP/IAP-NNF + AIIMS Indian clinical overlay  •  Current authoritative international protocols"}, {"t": "p", "html": "75 base MCQs + 3 separately scored notebook retests (Q1–Q78)  |  6 visual stations  |  8 question-linked visual/data items  |  No calculation drill on odd-numbered Day 11"}, {"t": "table", "head": ["<strong>Role</strong>", "<strong>Reference</strong>", "<strong>How it is used in this document</strong>"], "rows": [["Primary conceptual framework", "Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22nd Edition", "Core pathophysiology, disease framework, examination concepts and broad management principles."], ["Neonatal bedside / management cross-reference", "Cloherty and Stark's Manual of Neonatal Care, 9th Edition (Hansen, Stark, Eichenwald, Martin; Wolters Kluwer/LWW, 2022)", "NICU-oriented evaluation, monitoring, stabilization and practical neonatal management cross-read. It does not override newer named guidelines."], ["Topic-specific depth", "Cloherty 9e: Chapter 54 Intracranial Hemorrhage and White Matter Injury/PVL; Chapter 67 Retinopathy of Prematurity; Chapter 68 Hearing Health in NICU Graduates", "Used as chapter-level cross-reading for today's IVH/PVL, ROP and hearing-follow-up topics."], ["Neurology depth", "Volpe's Neurology of the Newborn", "Mechanism and neuroimaging depth for GMH-IVH, PVHI, PHVD and white-matter injury."], ["Current protocol layer", "ICROP3, ETROP/AAP, NHM India, CPS, JCIH, ELVIS and other named current guidance", "Current thresholds, screening intervals, treatment criteria and follow-up rules. When these differ from textbook/manual frameworks, the named current protocol is stated explicitly."], ["India-specific paediatric reference", "Indian Academy of Pediatrics (IAP) Standard Treatment Guidelines", "Use current IAP topic-specific guidance for Indian exam/practice context. Day 11 particularly uses IAP STG 144 Retinopathy of Prematurity and IAP STG 123 Early Detection and Early Intervention of a High-Risk Neonate."], ["India-specific neonatal reference", "IAP–NNF Quick Reference Guidelines in Neonatology 2026", "Use the relevant neonatal quick-reference topics, especially Preterm Brain Injury and Neuroprotection and Posthemorrhagic Hydrocephalus in Preterm Neonates, plus oxygen/pain/cord-management guidance where relevant."], ["India tertiary-NICU practical reference", "AIIMS Protocols in Neonatology / WHO Collaborating Centre for Training and Research in Newborn Care, AIIMS New Delhi", "Use for Indian tertiary-NICU bedside algorithms, ROP prevention/screening/treatment logistics, preterm stabilization, oxygen administration, pain management and follow-up; adapt to local setting as AIIMS itself advises."]]}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source-discipline rule: Nelson 22e remains the primary conceptual source. Cloherty &amp; Stark 9e provides neonatal bedside/management cross-reference. IAP and IAP–NNF guidance provide India-specific paediatric/neonatal exam and practice framing. AIIMS neonatal protocols provide tertiary-NICU practical context. Current named protocols (NHM, ICROP3, ETROP/AAP, CPS, JCIH, ELVIS and others) control current thresholds/schedules when newer than textbooks/manuals. Recommendations are labelled by framework and are not silently blended."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Clinical education only. Guideline cutoffs and follow-up intervals are explicitly source-labelled; unit protocols and specialist judgment govern patient care."}]}, {"title": "Today’S Learning Objectives", "h1": "Today’S Learning Objectives", "blocks": [{"t": "list", "ordered": false, "items": ["Interpret ICROP3 zone, stage, plus/preplus and aggressive ROP; decide Type 1 treatment versus Type 2 surveillance.", "Use Indian NHM and AAP screening rules without silently mixing eligibility and first-exam dates.", "Grade GMH–IVH, recognize PVHI and evolving PVL on serial cranial ultrasound, and escalate PHVD using measurements.", "Design corrected-age, vision, hearing, motor, cognitive and language follow-up; apply R1/R2/R3 to documented notebook errors."]}]}]}, {"id": "part-1", "title": "Conceptual & Clinical Notes", "eyebrow": "Part A", "group": "Study Notes", "kind": "notes", "sections": [{"title": "A1. ROP: pathogenesis, classification and visual recognition", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "The immature retinal vascular network normally advances from posterior to peripheral retina. Premature birth interrupts this process; oxygen fluctuations, VEGF suppression and later hypoxia-driven angiogenesis can produce ROP. Oxygen exposure is a modifiable contributor, not a necessary condition. Lower gestational age, poor postnatal growth, severe illness, sepsis and transfusion correlate with risk; the magnitude and causality of individual associations vary."}, {"t": "table", "head": ["<strong>Descriptor</strong>", "<strong>Exam anchor</strong>", "<strong>Discriminator</strong>"], "rows": [["Zone I", "Circle centered on disc, radius twice disc–macula distance", "Most posterior; severe disease can require treatment without plus."], ["Zone II", "Annulus from zone I to nasal ora serrata", "Zone II stage 3 without plus is Type 2."], ["Zone III", "Residual temporal crescent", "Usually lower risk, but not an automatic stopping rule."], ["Stage 1", "Flat demarcation line", "No elevated ridge."], ["Stage 2", "Elevated ridge", "No extraretinal neovascularization."], ["Stage 3", "Extraretinal neovascular proliferation, including flat lesions", "Can be present without a classic ridge."], ["Stage 4A / 4B", "Partial detachment without / with foveal involvement", "Foveal status changes prognosis."], ["Stage 5A / 5B / 5C", "Total detachment: open funnel with disc visible / closed funnel / total detachment with anterior-segment changes", "ICROP3 subdivisions refine anatomy and prognosis; all are advanced disease."], ["Plus / preplus", "Posterior dilation+tortuosity; spectrum under ICROP3", "Plus changes Type 1 thresholds; preplus is not automatically plus."], ["A-ROP", "Rapid progression, often flat posterior neovascularization and severe plus", "May bypass orderly stages."]]}, {"t": "p", "html": "TYPE 1 (treatment-requiring, ETROP): zone I any stage WITH plus; zone I stage 3 WITHOUT plus; zone II stage 2 or 3 WITH plus. TYPE 2 (close observation): zone I stage 1 or 2 without plus; zone II stage 3 without plus. Timing: specialist treatment ideally within about 72 hours of Type 1 confirmation; clinical urgency may be greater for A-ROP."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Laser ablates avascular peripheral retina and remains a durable standard for much zone II treatment, but can increase myopia and peripheral field loss. Anti-VEGF is especially useful in very posterior Zone I/A-ROP or when laser is technically difficult; persistent avascular retina and late reactivation require prolonged surveillance. In Indian practice, bevacizumab is widely discussed and may be used off-label in selected tertiary centres because of familiarity and lower cost, whereas ranibizumab/aflibercept availability and cost vary. NHM operational material does not recommend routine neonatal bevacizumab; all anti-VEGF use is specialist-led with informed consent, systemic-safety counselling and prolonged follow-up. Propranolol is NOT routine ROP prophylaxis or treatment: evidence is inconsistent and systemic adverse effects include bradycardia, hypotension, bronchospasm and hypoglycemia."}]}, {"title": "A1A. ROP trial anchors — know the question each trial answered", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "ETROP established the Type 1 treatment framework for high-risk prethreshold ROP. BEAT-ROP found bevacizumab superior to conventional laser for recurrence in Zone I stage 3+ disease, without the same clear advantage in Zone II. RAINBOW supported ranibizumab as an effective anti-VEGF option compared with laser, and FIREFLEYE supported aflibercept efficacy in treatment-requiring ROP. These trials do NOT make anti-VEGF universally superior: posterior anatomy, recurrence/reactivation profile, systemic-safety uncertainty, follow-up reliability, licensing and local access all matter."}, {"t": "img", "src": "img/image10.png", "alt": ""}, {"t": "p", "html": "<em>Purpose-built educational comparison; agent choice and dosing are specialist/protocol dependent.</em>"}]}, {"title": "A2. Screening — India and international protocols must remain separate", "blocks": [{"t": "table", "head": ["<strong>Framework</strong>", "<strong>Eligibility</strong>", "<strong>First examination</strong>", "<strong>Follow-up / stopping</strong>"], "rows": [["India NHM / operational Indian exam anchor", "GA ≤34 weeks OR BW ≤2000 g is the widely quoted Indian operational/exam anchor; 34–36 weeks with risk factors and unstable high-risk infants are also screened. Important source-version caveat: the currently linked revised NHM PDF uses the words '&lt;34 weeks' and '&lt;2000 g', while the older NHM 'Final' summary and Indian operational summaries use '≤'. Do not construct or answer a boundary-only stem without naming the source version.", "By about 4 weeks; very high-risk infants (&lt;28 weeks or &lt;1200 g in NHM text) at 2–3 weeks.", "Document zone/stage/plus and the exact next date; missed follow-up is a preventable cause of blindness."], ["AAP 2018 US framework", "BW ≤1500 g OR GA ≤30 weeks; selected 1500–2000 g or older unstable infants", "PMA- and GA-based table: 24-week infant → 31 weeks PMA (7 weeks PNA); 28-week infant → 32 weeks PMA (4 weeks PNA)", "Zone I stage 1–2 usually ≤1 week; zone II stage 2 1–2 weeks; zone II stage 1 ~2 weeks; post anti-VEGF surveillance often ≥65 weeks PMA."]]}, {"t": "p", "html": "EXAM/PRACTICE DIFFERENCE: Indian sources are not perfectly uniform at the exact 34-week/2000-g boundary. The older NHM final summary and Indian operational review use ≤34 weeks and ≤2000 g, while the currently linked revised NHM PDF text says &lt;34 weeks and &lt;2000 g. For NEET-SS, use the explicitly named source/framework and avoid boundary-only reasoning unless the stem specifies the document. AAP 2018 remains a separate US framework with its own eligibility and PMA-based first-exam table."}]}, {"title": "A3. ROP decision algorithm", "blocks": [{"t": "list", "ordered": true, "items": ["Identify eligibility and ensure a named ophthalmologist and dated first exam.", "At each exam record zone, stage, extent, plus/preplus, A-ROP, regression and treatment history.", "Type 1 or A-ROP → urgent retina specialist treatment assessment; document treatment date.", "Type 2 → close interval follow-up by zone/stage (often ≤1 week posteriorly).", "After laser → early review (often 3–7 days) and confirm regression; after anti-VEGF → extended reactivation surveillance.", "After acute ROP resolution → assess refractive error, amblyopia, strabismus and visual development."]}]}, {"title": "A3A. ROP examination practicalities — high-yield bedside safety", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "NHM screening guidance emphasizes comfort, monitoring and asepsis. Before examination: confirm consent, warm/swaddle the infant, dilate pupils with the correct neonatal preparation, and use pulse-oximetry for sick infants. Topical proparacaine is used before the speculum; oral sucrose may be considered for analgesia. During the exam, monitor heart rate and oxygen saturation and interrupt the procedure for significant bradycardia, apnea or severe desaturation until recovery. Use sterile instruments for each infant with hand hygiene/glove change between examinations. Wide-field digital imaging can support documentation and tele-ROP pathways, especially where ophthalmology access is limited, but NHM guidance treats it as an adjunct rather than a complete replacement for expert indirect ophthalmoscopy when image quality or disease severity is uncertain."}, {"t": "p", "html": "AIIMS practical cross-read: for ROP treatment, AIIMS protocols emphasize cardiorespiratory monitoring and adequate analgesia/sedation; topical anesthesia alone is insufficient for treatment procedures such as laser. AIIMS also stresses prolonged observation after sedative/analgesic regimens and contextual adaptation to unit resources. This is a treatment-procedure issue and should not be confused with the simpler comfort measures used for routine screening examinations."}, {"t": "img", "src": "img/image7.png", "alt": ""}, {"t": "p", "html": "<em>Purpose-built educational workflow; not a patient image.</em>"}]}, {"title": "A4. Germinal-matrix hemorrhage, IVH, PVHI and cranial ultrasound", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Fragile germinal-matrix vessels at the caudothalamic groove rupture especially in the first 72 hours of extreme prematurity. Hemodynamic instability, fluctuating cerebral perfusion, coagulopathy and very low gestation increase risk. Serial cranial ultrasound detects GMH–IVH, evolving ventricular enlargement and cystic white-matter injury. Mastoid views improve posterior-fossa detection."}, {"t": "table", "head": ["<strong>Pattern</strong>", "<strong>Imaging finding</strong>", "<strong>Interpretation</strong>"], "rows": [["Grade I", "Hemorrhage confined to caudothalamic germinal matrix", "No ventricular blood."], ["Grade II", "Intraventricular blood WITHOUT dilatation", "Serial scans needed for evolution."], ["Grade III", "Intraventricular blood WITH ventricular dilatation", "Higher PHVD risk."], ["PVHI (old “grade IV”)", "Unilateral/asymmetric wedge-shaped parenchymal hemorrhagic venous infarction", "Not simply ventricular blood extending into brain."], ["Cystic PVL", "Delayed bilateral periventricular cysts after persistent echogenicity", "White-matter injury, not an IVH grade."], ["Noncystic WMI", "Subtle diffuse white-matter injury; MRI may detect more than US", "Normal US does not exclude this pattern."]]}, {"t": "p", "html": "CPS (reaffirmed 12 Jan 2026): routine HUS for ≤31+6 weeks at 4–7 days and again 4–6 weeks; grade II+ IVH or WMI → repeat 7–10 days; progressive ventricular dilation → at least weekly initially and often more frequently. At &lt;26 weeks, term-equivalent HUS is advised. For 32–36+6 weeks, selective imaging is appropriate with severe illness or neurologic risk."}]}, {"title": "A5. PHVD: serial measurement and escalation", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "PHVD can progress before head circumference accelerates or the fontanelle bulges. Measure ventricular index (VI), anterior horn width (AHW), and thalamo-occipital distance (TOD) on consistent serial planes. ELVIS low threshold: VI &gt;97th centile with AHW &gt;6 mm and/or TOD &gt;25 mm; high threshold: VI &gt;97th centile +4 mm with AHW &gt;10 mm. These are trial pathways, not universal automatic-drainage orders. In some published pathways, 2–3 lumbar punctures aim to remove about 10 mL/kg CSF; this is protocol-dependent, not a universal volume prescription. Progressive disease may require a ventricular reservoir or ventriculosubgaleal shunt, with VP shunt timing individualized by weight, CSF quality, ventricular trajectory, infection status and local neurosurgical practice."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Evidence calibration: the original ELVIS primary composite (death or VP shunt) was not significantly different between randomized thresholds, but MRI and later developmental analyses favored earlier measurement-led intervention. The 2026 Pediatric Research Bayesian reanalysis is now verified: delaying intervention had &gt;90% posterior probability of harm for death or moderate/severe neurodevelopmental impairment, with an estimated absolute risk difference of 16% and NNT about 7 under the reported analysis. This strengthens the case for early ultrasound-guided escalation, but does not make every low-threshold crossing an automatic operation."}]}, {"title": "A5A. PHVD drainage safety and permanent-shunt caveats", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "IAP–NNF 2026 cross-read: 'Posthemorrhagic Hydrocephalus in Preterm Neonates' is the preferred current Indian quick-reference overlay for practical escalation. AIIMS neonatal practice resources are used for NICU execution and transfer/neurosurgical coordination. ELVIS/Volpe remain the evidence/imaging anchors for VI/AHW/TOD interpretation."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Temporizing drainage is not benign. Reservoir/VSGS pathways require strict asepsis, trained operators and slow controlled removal; one review recommends reservoir drainage no faster than about 1 mL/min. Important complications include infection, obstruction, CSF leak, skin problems, revision and rare intracranial hemorrhage. Repeated drainage is burdensome and removes protein-containing CSF, so prolonged pathways require attention to nutrition, electrolytes and overall growth. A CSF protein threshold such as &lt;1.5 g/L before VP shunting appears in some published pathways, but it is NOT a universal criterion and should be presented only as a centre-/study-dependent example, not an exam-wide rule."}, {"t": "img", "src": "img/image9.png", "alt": ""}, {"t": "p", "html": "<em>Purpose-built educational PHVD escalation schematic; not a patient record.</em>"}]}, {"title": "A6. PVL and long-term developmental sequelae", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Periventricular white-matter injury may follow inflammation, ischemia, infection or hemodynamic instability. Persistent periventricular echogenicity can precede cystic PVL at 2–6 weeks; small cysts may later resolve, leaving volume loss or ex-vacuo ventriculomegaly. MRI at term-equivalent age is more sensitive for noncystic WMI but is selective, not mandatory for every preterm infant. Cystic PVL raises risk of spastic diplegia; cerebellar injury and diffuse WMI may contribute to motor, cognitive, language and executive-function difficulties."}]}, {"title": "A7. Neurodevelopmental follow-up — Nelson 22e framework; Cloherty & Stark 9e bedside follow-up cross-read; JCIH/current protocols for hearing", "blocks": [{"t": "table", "head": ["<strong>Domain</strong>", "<strong>What to assess</strong>", "<strong>Escalation trigger</strong>"], "rows": [["Motor", "Tone, posture, asymmetry, General Movements Assessment (fidgety 3–5 months CA) and HINE when trained examiners are available.", "Absent fidgety movements, persistent asymmetry/scissoring, or low HINE (e.g. &lt;57 at 3–4 months in one large cohort) → early CP pathway; do not wait for a definitive label."], ["Cognition/language", "Communication, attention, problem solving, Bayley-type standardized assessment where available", "Regression, persistent language delay, poor interaction."], ["Hearing", "Newborn screen plus JCIH risk-factor surveillance; NICU stay &gt;5 days warrants comprehensive follow-up by about 9 months even after an earlier pass.", "Any caregiver concern, speech/language delay, ototoxic exposure or inconsistent sound response → diagnostic audiology."], ["Vision", "ROP completion + refraction, strabismus, amblyopia and cerebral visual function after PVL/WMI.", "Visual inattention, crowding/face-recognition difficulty, high refractive error, strabismus or late ROP reactivation."], ["Feeding/growth", "Growth trajectory, oral motor feeding, micronutrient adequacy", "Aspiration signs, poor weight gain, oral aversion."], ["Family / services", "Caregiver concerns, accessible early intervention, follow-up attendance", "Any red flag; do not wait for definitive CP diagnosis."]]}, {"t": "p", "html": "Corrected age = chronological age − (40 weeks − gestational age at birth), typically used for developmental interpretation through approximately 24 months. PMA = GA at birth + postnatal age; it is used for neonatal schedules and is not corrected age. Prognosis is probabilistic: combine imaging, neurologic examination, clinical course and repeated developmental assessment."}]}, {"title": "A7A. Early CP, hearing and vision follow-up — calibrated thresholds", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "IAP overlay: use IAP STG 123 'Early Detection and Early Intervention of a High-Risk Neonate' for India-specific surveillance/referral framing. This complements, rather than replaces, validated tools such as GMA/HINE and current JCIH hearing-risk guidance."}, {"t": "p", "html": "General Movements Assessment (especially absent fidgety movements at 3–5 months corrected age), HINE and neuroimaging can identify high risk for cerebral palsy before 6 months corrected age. A HINE total score &lt;57 at 3–4 months corrected age had strong predictive performance in a large high-risk cohort; a 2026 meta-analysis used &lt;58 at 3 months. Treat these as study-derived predictive cut-offs, not a stand-alone diagnostic definition of CP. JCIH 2019/2024 FAQ guidance supports risk-factor audiology follow-up by about 9 months for infants with NICU stay &gt;5 days even if an earlier diagnostic assessment was passed; additional follow-up is individualized. Visual follow-up should include refraction, strabismus/amblyopia and cerebral visual impairment, especially after PVL/WMI."}]}, {"title": "A8. Management, complications, evidence levels", "blocks": [{"t": "list", "ordered": false, "items": ["ROP: prevention includes safe oxygen targeting, avoidance of unnecessary hyperoxia and reliable screening; no single oxygen target eliminates risk, and overly restrictive oxygenation may increase mortality. Anti-VEGF long-term systemic safety is still an area of uncertainty.", "IVH prevention/risk modification: antenatal corticosteroids and gentle delivery-room/cardiorespiratory stabilization are well-supported. Avoid large or rapid volume boluses, major blood-pressure swings, large PaCO₂ fluctuations and unnecessarily aggressive ventilation because unstable cerebral blood flow increases hemorrhage risk. Hypotension, a hemodynamically important PDA, sepsis and ventilator-related CO₂ swings are clinical risk modifiers, but association does not prove that routine PDA closure prevents IVH. Delayed cord clamping is recommended where appropriate for preterm transition and has clearer evidence for mortality/transfusion benefit; its specific effect on severe IVH is mixed/low-certainty and should not be overstated. Routine indomethacin prophylaxis reduces severe IVH in some trials but has not shown a corresponding improvement in survival free of neurodevelopmental disability and remains unit-dependent.", "PVL/PHVD: monitor early complications (seizures, ventricular enlargement, feeding instability) and later motor/cognitive outcomes; prompt imaging-based referral can prevent delayed intervention.", "India overlay: follow national NHM screening and locally available NNF/IAP/AIIMS neurodevelopment services; exact ultrasound follow-up intervals and rehabilitation access may vary by unit. The international CPS schedule is presented as a named exam framework, not as an Indian national mandate."]}]}, {"title": "A9. Integrated management algorithms", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "ROP pathway: screen → zone/stage/plus/A-ROP → Type 1 (urgent treat) OR Type 2 (close surveillance) → confirm regression → late reactivation/refractive follow-up."}, {"t": "p", "html": "IVH pathway: stabilize cardiorespiratory physiology → bedside HUS → grade GMH/IVH and describe PVHI separately → repeat at 7–10 days if grade II+ → serial VI/AHW/TOD if PHVD → neurosurgical discussion."}, {"t": "p", "html": "PVL pathway: first HUS → repeat 4–6 weeks after birth / after severe illness → persistent echogenicity/cysts → selective MRI if needed → early intervention and longitudinal development surveillance."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Discharge pathway: written ROP appointment + cranial imaging plan + corrected-age high-risk clinic + hearing/vision/feeding assessment + family contact and missed-appointment recovery."}, {"t": "img", "src": "img/image1.png", "alt": ""}, {"t": "p", "html": "<em>Purpose-built decision-flow schematic; educational only.</em>"}]}]}, {"id": "data", "title": "Data Interpretation", "group": "Practice", "kind": "data", "sections": [{"title": "Visual Station 1 — Line versus ridge (Visual A)", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Question: A fundus-like teaching image shows a peripheral junctional lesion. Which panel is stage 1 and which is stage 2?"}, {"t": "img", "src": "img/image2_q.png", "alt": ""}, {"t": "img", "src": "img/image2.png", "alt": "", "reveal": true, "caption": "Labelled schematic from the source document"}, {"t": "p", "html": "<em>Purpose-built educational schematic; not a clinical patient image or recording.</em>"}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Interpretation: </strong>Left = stage 1 demarcation line; right = stage 2 elevated ridge."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Decisive visual/data features: </strong>The right panel has visible elevated junctional tissue; the left has a thin demarcation only."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Closest distractor / discriminator: </strong>Extraretinal neovascular proliferation, not just a thicker ridge, would make stage 3."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Next management step: </strong>Record full zone/stage/plus; schedule review rather than treating stage alone."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Exam trap: </strong>A ridge does not automatically mean Type 1 ROP."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Trace-back: </strong>A1; Q1, Q2, Q31, Q51."}]}, {"title": "Visual Station 2 — Plus spectrum and aggressive ROP (Visual B)", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Question: The right panel has very tortuous dilated vessels and flat posterior neovascularization. What risk phenotype is suggested?"}, {"t": "img", "src": "img/image3_q.png", "alt": ""}, {"t": "img", "src": "img/image3.png", "alt": "", "reveal": true, "caption": "Labelled schematic from the source document"}, {"t": "p", "html": "<em>Purpose-built educational schematic; not a clinical patient image or recording.</em>"}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Interpretation: </strong>Aggressive ROP phenotype, requiring urgent specialist assessment."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Decisive visual/data features: </strong>Marked posterior vascular dilation/tortuosity with flat neovascular tissue and rapid progression."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Closest distractor / discriminator: </strong>Preplus has vascular change below the plus threshold; an ordinary stage 2 ridge alone lacks flat neovascular proliferation."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Next management step: </strong>Urgent Type 1/A-ROP treatment-oriented ophthalmic evaluation."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Exam trap: </strong>Do not demand orderly stage 1→2→3 progression in aggressive ROP."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Trace-back: </strong>A1/A3; Q22, Q48, Q50."}]}, {"title": "Visual Station 3 — Grade I/II/III versus PVHI (Visual C)", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Question: Compare the four cranial US panels. Which one has a parenchymal venous infarct rather than only ventricular hemorrhage?"}, {"t": "img", "src": "img/image4_q.png", "alt": ""}, {"t": "img", "src": "img/image4.png", "alt": "", "reveal": true, "caption": "Labelled schematic from the source document"}, {"t": "p", "html": "<em>Purpose-built educational schematic; not a clinical patient image or recording.</em>"}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Interpretation: </strong>Bottom right demonstrates PVHI; top left grade I; top right grade II; bottom left grade III."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Decisive visual/data features: </strong>PVHI adds a wedge-like echogenic periventricular lesion to ventricular hemorrhage."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Closest distractor / discriminator: </strong>Grade III is ventricular blood with dilation but lacks a parenchymal wedge."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Next management step: </strong>Repeat HUS and monitor PHVD; involve neonatal neurology as indicated."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Exam trap: </strong>“Grade IV IVH” obscures PVHI mechanism."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Trace-back: </strong>A4; Q5, Q33–36, Q55."}]}, {"title": "Visual Station 4 — Ventricular enlargement and ELVIS cutoffs (Visual D)", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Question: The right panel shows enlarged ventricles. Which quantitative indices and thresholds change management?"}, {"t": "img", "src": "img/image5_q.png", "alt": ""}, {"t": "img", "src": "img/image5.png", "alt": "", "reveal": true, "caption": "Labelled schematic from the source document"}, {"t": "p", "html": "<em>Purpose-built educational schematic; not a clinical patient image or recording.</em>"}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Interpretation: </strong>Use serial VI, AHW, TOD; ELVIS low VI&gt;p97, AHW&gt;6 mm and/or TOD&gt;25 mm; high VI&gt;p97+4 mm and AHW&gt;10 mm."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Decisive visual/data features: </strong>Visible ventricular expansion must be quantified consistently on serial planes."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Closest distractor / discriminator: </strong>A soft fontanelle or normal head circumference does not exclude progressive PHVD."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Next management step: </strong>Increase scan frequency and discuss early neurosurgical pathway if progressive."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Exam trap: </strong>A single enlarged ventricle without trajectory/context does not dictate shunt placement."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Trace-back: </strong>A5; Q41, Q59–61, Q73."}]}, {"title": "Visual Station 5 — Evolving PVL on serial US (Visual E)", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Question: Why might a day-5 ultrasound be normal or show only echogenicity while a 5-week scan shows cysts?"}, {"t": "img", "src": "img/image6_q.png", "alt": ""}, {"t": "img", "src": "img/image6.png", "alt": "", "reveal": true, "caption": "Labelled schematic from the source document"}, {"t": "p", "html": "<em>Purpose-built educational schematic; not a clinical patient image or recording.</em>"}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Interpretation: </strong>Cystic PVL can evolve over 2–6 weeks after an inflammatory/ischemic insult."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Decisive visual/data features: </strong>Later bilateral periventricular cystic spaces differ from early echogenic flare."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Closest distractor / discriminator: </strong>Grade I GMH is caudothalamic, while PVL is white-matter injury adjacent to ventricles."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Next management step: </strong>Arrange high-risk developmental follow-up, motor assessment and early intervention."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Exam trap: </strong>No cysts on day 5 does not exclude later PVL."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Trace-back: </strong>A4/A6; Q37, Q43, Q68."}]}, {"title": "Visual Station 6 — ROP examination safety and tele-ROP workflow", "blocks": [{"t": "img", "src": "img/image7.png", "alt": "", "reveal": true, "caption": "Labelled schematic from the source document"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Purpose-built educational workflow; not a patient image or recording."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Question: During an ROP examination a preterm infant develops bradycardia and marked desaturation. What should happen immediately, and what measures reduce recurrence?"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Interpretation: Stop/pause the eye examination, allow recovery and stabilize cardiorespiratory status before deciding whether to resume."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Decisive visual/data features: ROP screening can provoke short-term HR/SpO₂ instability; continuous monitoring matters in sick infants."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Closest distractor / discriminator: Completing the retinal view is important, but it never outranks immediate physiologic stability."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Next management step: Reassess oxygenation/HR, optimize swaddling/analgesia, and resume only when stable with trained staff present."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Exam trap: Wide-field imaging can support tele-ROP, but image transfer does not remove the need for expert interpretation or bedside safety precautions."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Trace-back: A3A; NHM ROP examination precautions."}]}]}, {"id": "quizzes", "title": "Quizzes", "group": "Practice", "kind": "quizzes", "sections": []}, {"id": "revision", "title": "Rapid Revision", "group": "Revise", "kind": "revision", "sections": [{"title": "", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "India reference rule: IAP/IAP–NNF = India-specific paediatric/neonatal guidance; AIIMS = tertiary-NICU practical protocols; NHM = national programme/screening framework. If they differ from an international protocol, answer according to the framework explicitly named in the stem."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Reference trace-back: Nelson 22e = conceptual base; Cloherty &amp; Stark 9e = NICU bedside/management cross-read; current named protocols = thresholds/schedules/treatment criteria."}, {"t": "list", "ordered": false, "items": ["ICROP3: line = stage 1; ridge = stage 2; extraretinal neovascularization = stage 3; partial retinal detachment sparing fovea = 4A, involving fovea = 4B; total = stage 5.", "ETROP Type 1: zone I any stage PLUS; zone I stage 3 NO PLUS; zone II stage 2/3 PLUS. Type 2: zone I stage 1/2 no plus; zone II stage 3 no plus.", "A-ROP: rapidly progressive disease, flat posterior neovascularization, often marked plus; classic sequential stages may be absent.", "India NHM screening: widely quoted Indian operational/exam anchor is GA ≤34 weeks OR BW ≤2000 g, plus larger/more mature at-risk infants; the currently linked revised NHM PDF uses '&lt;34/&lt;2000' wording. Exact 34-week/2000-g equality is source-version-dependent, so use the named document. Very high-risk timing remains 2–3 weeks for &lt;28 weeks or &lt;1200 g in NHM text.", "AAP 2018: ≤1500 g or ≤30 weeks plus selected unstable larger/older infants; first examination per PMA/GA table. Do not import AAP timing into NHM questions.", "Anti-VEGF: posterior disease option; regression may not mean peripheral vascular maturity. Late recurrence demands prolonged follow-up (AAP 2018 anchor ≥65 weeks PMA).", "GMH at caudothalamic groove. Grade I confined GMH; II intraventricular blood no dilation; III blood with dilation; PVHI is parenchymal venous infarction, not merely grade IV extension.", "CPS reaffirmed 2026: ≤31+6 weeks → HUS at 4–7 days and 4–6 weeks; grade II+ IVH or WMI → repeat in 7–10 days; term-equivalent HUS for &lt;26 weeks.", "PHVD: serial VI, AHW, TOD. ELVIS low VI&gt;p97 + AHW&gt;6 and/or TOD&gt;25; high VI&gt;p97+4 mm + AHW&gt;10. Clinical pressure signs can lag.", "PVL: early persistent periventricular echogenicity; cysts may appear 2–6 weeks later. MRI better for noncystic WMI; cystic PVL raises risk of spastic diplegia.", "Corrected age = PNA − (40−GA); PMA = GA + PNA. Corrected age commonly used through 24 months for milestone interpretation.", "High-risk follow-up: motor tone/asymmetry, vision, hearing, language/cognition, growth/feeding, family concerns; early therapy does not require definitive CP diagnosis."]}, {"t": "p", "html": "Early CP/hearing: HINE &lt;57 at 3–4 months is a study-derived predictive cut-off; JCIH risk-factor follow-up by about 9 months remains appropriate after NICU &gt;5 days even after an earlier pass."}, {"t": "p", "html": "PHVD: ~10 mL/kg LP targets and CSF protein &lt;1.5 g/L before VP shunt are examples from some published pathways, not universal rules. Drainage requires strict sterility and slow controlled removal."}, {"t": "p", "html": "IVH prevention: delayed cord clamping has stronger mortality/transfusion evidence than a specific severe-IVH prevention effect; avoid overstating IVH benefit. Avoid BP/PaCO₂ swings and unnecessary rapid boluses."}, {"t": "p", "html": "ROP pharmacology: propranolol is not routine. Bevacizumab use in Indian tertiary practice is off-label/specialist-led; ranibizumab/aflibercept access and cost vary. Anti-VEGF requires prolonged reactivation surveillance."}, {"t": "p", "html": "ROP examination safety: topical anesthetic + swaddling ± sucrose, sterile instruments, pulse-oximetry for sick infants; interrupt for significant bradycardia/apnea/desaturation. Tele-ROP is an adjunct, not a universal replacement for indirect ophthalmoscopy."}, {"t": "list", "ordered": false, "items": ["Notebook carryover: DOPE circuit disconnection = Equipment; Jensen BPD noninvasive positive pressure at 36 weeks PMA = grade 2; DAT-negative indirect jaundice can indicate G6PD deficiency."]}]}]}, {"id": "recall", "title": "Active Recall", "group": "Revise", "kind": "recall", "sections": [{"title": "", "blocks": [{"t": "list", "ordered": true, "items": ["List all three ETROP Type 1 ROP categories.<br>Answer: Zone I any stage with plus; zone I stage 3 without plus; zone II stage 2 or 3 with plus.", "What makes stage 1, 2 and 3 morphologically different?<br>Answer: Stage 1 line; stage 2 elevated ridge; stage 3 extraretinal neovascular proliferation (including flat).", "What separates stage 4A from 4B?<br>Answer: 4A spares the fovea; 4B involves the fovea.", "How does ICROP3 treat plus versus preplus?<br>Answer: A posterior vessel dilation/tortuosity continuum; preplus does not automatically equal ETROP plus.", "Which Type 2 patterns demand close follow-up?<br>Answer: Zone I stage 1/2 without plus; zone II stage 3 without plus.", "Which India NHM infants qualify for ROP screening?<br>Answer: Indian operational exam anchor is commonly written GA ≤34 weeks or BW ≤2000 g, but the current revised NHM PDF text uses &lt;34/&lt;2000; name the source if the equality boundary matters.", "What is the Indian first-screen rule for &lt;28 weeks or &lt;1200 g?<br>Answer: By 2–3 weeks of postnatal life.", "AAP 2018: first exam for 24-week infant?<br>Answer: 31 weeks PMA, approximately 7 weeks PNA.", "What is the anti-VEGF surveillance trap?<br>Answer: Late reactivation/persistent avascular retina; follow-up often to ≥65 weeks PMA under AAP 2018.", "Where is the germinal matrix on ultrasound?<br>Answer: At the caudothalamic groove.", "Define grade I/II/III IVH.<br>Answer: I confined GMH; II ventricular blood without dilatation; III ventricular blood with dilatation.", "What is PVHI and why avoid “grade IV IVH”?<br>Answer: Hemorrhagic venous parenchymal infarction, often unilateral wedge-shaped, not simple ventricular blood extension.", "CPS 2026 HUS schedule for ≤31+6 weeks?<br>Answer: First at 4–7 days; repeat at 4–6 weeks; grade II+ IVH/WMI → repeat 7–10 days.", "Which infant routinely needs term-equivalent HUS under CPS?<br>Answer: Born &lt;26 weeks; others if moderate/severe abnormalities or risk factors.", "State VI, AHW and TOD.<br>Answer: Ventricular index; anterior horn width; thalamo-occipital distance.", "State ELVIS low and high PHVD thresholds.<br>Answer: Low VI&gt;p97 and AHW&gt;6 mm and/or TOD&gt;25 mm; high VI&gt;p97+4 mm and AHW&gt;10 mm.", "Why should PHVD not await a bulging fontanelle?<br>Answer: Preterm skull compliance makes clinical signs late; serial US detects expansion earlier.", "When do PVL cysts typically appear?<br>Answer: Over about 2–6 weeks after injury; a day-5 scan may miss them.", "Which modality better detects noncystic WMI?<br>Answer: MRI, selectively at term-equivalent age in high-risk infants.", "What is the typical motor phenotype after cystic PVL?<br>Answer: Increased risk of spastic diplegia; outcome is not certain from imaging alone.", "Calculate corrected age for GA 27 weeks at 16 weeks PNA.<br>Answer: 16−(40−27)=3 weeks corrected age.", "What is PMA for GA 24 weeks at 7 weeks PNA?<br>Answer: 31 weeks PMA.", "Visual pattern: what separates stage 1 from stage 2 in Visual A?<br>Answer: A flat line versus an elevated ridge.", "Visual pattern: what indicates aggressive ROP in Visual B?<br>Answer: Marked plus-spectrum tortuosity with flat neovascularization and rapid progression.", "Visual pattern: what identifies PVHI in Visual C?<br>Answer: A unilateral/asymmetric wedge-like parenchymal echogenic lesion accompanying IVH.", "Visual pattern: what serial change indicates PVL in Visual E?<br>Answer: Persistent echogenicity evolving into periventricular cysts weeks later.", "Notebook recall: in DOPE, a disconnected ventilator circuit is which component?<br>Answer: Equipment failure.", "Notebook recall: Jensen BPD on noninvasive positive-pressure support at 36 weeks PMA?<br>Answer: Grade 2.", "Notebook recall: DAT-negative severe unconjugated jaundice with modest anemia suggests?<br>Answer: G6PD deficiency among the key nonimmune causes.", "What is the NHM 34-week/2000-g boundary caveat across Indian source versions?<br>Answer: Older NHM/Indian operational summaries use ≤34 weeks or ≤2000 g; the currently linked revised NHM PDF text uses &lt;34/&lt;2000. Name the source and avoid equality-boundary-only reasoning.", "Name four practical measures that reduce pain/instability during ROP screening.<br>Answer: Pulse-oximetry/physiologic monitoring, swaddling, topical proparacaine, oral sucrose if used locally, sterile instruments/hand hygiene, and interrupting the exam for significant bradycardia/apnea/desaturation.", "What is the role of tele-ROP/wide-field digital imaging in current Indian guidance?<br>Answer: Wide-field imaging is useful for documentation and telemedicine/remote expert review, but NHM treats it as an adjunct rather than a full replacement for expert indirect ophthalmoscopy in all cases.", "Is propranolol routine prevention or treatment for ROP?<br>Answer: No. Evidence remains insufficient/inconsistent for routine propranolol and adverse effects are clinically important.", "How should delayed cord clamping be worded with respect to IVH prevention?<br>Answer: DCC is appropriate for preterm transition and has stronger mortality/transfusion evidence; a specific reduction in severe IVH is mixed/low-certainty and should not be overclaimed.", "What does a HINE score &lt;57 at 3–4 months corrected age mean?<br>Answer: It is a study-derived high-risk predictive cut-off for CP, not a stand-alone diagnostic definition; combine GMA, HINE, imaging and serial examination.", "What JCIH follow-up is recommended for an infant with NICU stay &gt;5 days who passed earlier testing?<br>Answer: Risk-factor audiologic follow-up by about 9 months is still advised; an earlier diagnostic test does not replace that surveillance.", "Which PHVD drainage/shunt numbers are protocol-dependent rather than universal?<br>Answer: Examples include ~10 mL/kg CSF removal in some LP pathways and CSF protein &lt;1.5 g/L in some shunt pathways; both are centre-/study-dependent, not universal."]}]}]}, {"id": "notebook", "title": "Error Notebook", "group": "Revise", "kind": "notebook", "sections": []}, {"id": "plan", "title": "Revision Plan", "group": "Revise", "kind": "plan", "sections": [{"title": "Error Notebook & Spaced Review Tracker", "h1": "Error Notebook & Spaced Review Tracker", "blocks": [{"t": "table", "head": ["<strong>Status</strong>", "<strong>Concept</strong>", "<strong>Action today</strong>", "<strong>Next</strong>"], "rows": [["R1 overdue", "ERR-D5-Q34 — DOPE / circuit disconnection", "Q16 extra; log correct/incorrect", "If passed, R2 ~7 days after R1; R3 ~21 days after R1."], ["R1 overdue", "ERR-D5-Q53 — Jensen BPD grade", "Q42 extra; log correct/incorrect", "Retain original failure count; advance only after successful fresh stem."], ["R1 overdue", "ERR-D6-Q60 — G6PD/DAT-negative jaundice", "Q63 extra; log correct/incorrect", "If failed, mark x2 and re-review mechanism."], ["R1 passed 8 Oct", "ERR-D7-Q34 — aEEG/raw EEG", "Not due today", "R2 approximately 13 Oct; R3 ~27 Oct."], ["R1 passed 8 Oct", "ERR-D8-Q41 — preterm energy/protein", "Not due today", "R2 approximately 14 Oct; R3 ~28 Oct."], ["R1 due 10–11 Oct", "ERR-D10-Q8/Q15/Q47/Q50 — 4 resolved concepts", "Reserved for user-selected window; not inserted early", "Fresh extra stems on Day 12 if still due."], ["Unresolved", "ERR-D5-Q4 original concept not recovered", "No invented retest", "Recover source question before insertion."], ["Unresolved", "ERR-D10-Q65 answer A→D; concept not verified", "No invented retest", "Recover original stem before insertion."]]}, {"t": "p", "html": "RAG: GREEN = fresh-stem correct with confident reasoning; AMBER = correct with hesitation or incomplete discriminator; RED = wrong, guessed or unable to justify. A repeated miss increments xTime of failure; passing R1 does not erase the original miss."}]}, {"title": "Self-Assessment", "h1": "Self-Assessment", "blocks": [{"t": "table", "head": ["<strong>Component</strong>", "<strong>Score</strong>", "<strong>RAG / action</strong>"], "rows": [["Base MCQs", "____ / 75", "GREEN ≥85%; AMBER 70–84%; RED &lt;70%. Record each difficulty bucket separately."], ["Notebook Retests — Extra", "____ / 3", "Separate from base score; each result determines R1→R2→R3 or x2 failure."], ["Visual stations", "____ / 6", "Explain decisive feature, nearest mimic, next management step and one safety/implementation point."], ["Active Recall", "____ / 37", "Repeat incorrect or hesitant thresholds, safety steps and image patterns."], ["Calculation Drill", "Not scheduled on Day 11", "Alternate-day rule: next extra drill on Day 12; no hidden numerical block today."]]}]}]}, {"id": "references", "title": "References", "group": "More", "kind": "notes", "sections": [{"title": "References / Protocol Source Labels", "h1": "References / Protocol Source Labels", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22nd Edition — primary conceptual reference (chapter-based clinical framework; specific page numbering depends on print/e-book edition)."}, {"t": "p", "html": "ICROP3 classification: Chiang MF et al. The International Classification of Retinopathy of Prematurity, Third Edition. Ophthalmology. 2021. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ophtha.2021.05.031"}, {"t": "p", "html": "AAP: Screening Examination of Premature Infants for Retinopathy of Prematurity. Pediatrics 2018. https://publications.aap.org/pediatrics/article/142/6/e20183061/37478/Screening-Examination-of-Premature-Infants-for"}, {"t": "p", "html": "NHM India: Guidelines for Universal Eye Screening Including ROP in Newborns. https://nhm.gov.in/images/pdf/programmes/RBSK/Resource_Documents/Revised_ROP_Guidelines-Web_Optimized.pdf"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Canadian Paediatric Society: Routine imaging of the preterm neonatal brain, 2020, reaffirmed 12 Jan 2026. https://cps.ca/en/documents/position/routine-imaging-of-preterm-neonatal-brain"}, {"t": "p", "html": "ELVIS trial thresholds: de Vries LS et al. Treatment thresholds for intervention in PHVD. Arch Dis Child Fetal Neonatal Ed. https://research.rug.nl/en/publications/treatment-thresholds-for-intervention-in-posthaemorrhagic-ventric/"}, {"t": "p", "html": "2026 ELVIS Bayesian reanalysis: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41390-026-05172-4"}, {"t": "p", "html": "ROP reactivation after anti-VEGF review, Jan 2026: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40889820/"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Propranolol for ROP: systematic reviews/meta-analyses show inconsistent efficacy and clinically relevant adverse events; routine use is not recommended as a standard ROP strategy."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Delayed cord clamping and severe IVH: 2025 systematic review/meta-analysis (PubMed 40009183) supports mortality benefit; evidence for a specific severe-IVH reduction is low/very-low certainty and should be described cautiously."}, {"t": "p", "html": "JCIH 2019 / 2024 Audiology FAQ: infants with NICU stay &gt;5 days require risk-factor follow-up by about 9 months even if earlier diagnostic testing was passed. https://www.jcih.org/docs/JCIH%20Frequently%20Asked%20Questions_Audiology%20%282024%29.pdf"}, {"t": "p", "html": "HINE evidence: HINE &lt;57 at 3–4 months corrected age showed strong CP prediction in a large high-risk cohort (PubMed 36126506); 2026 meta-analysis used &lt;58 at 3 months (PubMed 42375103)."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Management of PHVD review: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8297821/ — examples include 2–3 LPs aiming ~10 mL/kg and strict reservoir-drainage safety; permanent shunt readiness is individualized."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Cizmeci MN et al. ELVIS Bayesian reanalysis. Pediatric Research. Published 9 Jun 2026. https://www.nature.com/articles/s41390-026-05172-4"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Canadian Paediatric Society. Routine imaging of the preterm neonatal brain. Reaffirmed 12 Jan 2026: HUS 4–7 days and 4–6 weeks for ≤31+6 weeks; repeat 7–10 days after grade II+ IVH/WMI; term-equivalent HUS for &lt;26 weeks."}, {"t": "p", "html": "NHM older Final ROP summary / Indian operational review: uses ≤34 weeks / ≤2000 g, explaining the boundary-language discrepancy. Operational review: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7001189/"}, {"t": "p", "html": "NHM India revised ROP operational guideline (current linked resource): https://nhm.gov.in/images/pdf/programmes/RBSK/Resource_Documents/Revised_ROP_Guidelines-Web_Optimized.pdf — text uses &lt;34 weeks / &lt;2000 g; includes examination precautions, sucrose, monitoring, asepsis and telemedicine adjuncts."}, {"t": "p", "html": "ETROP Cooperative Group — early treatment of high-risk prethreshold ROP established the Type 1 framework."}, {"t": "p", "html": "BEAT-ROP — intravitreal bevacizumab versus laser for stage 3+ ROP; key exam signal is the Zone I recurrence advantage."}, {"t": "p", "html": "RAINBOW — ranibizumab compared with laser in ROP; effective anti-VEGF option with ongoing long-term surveillance considerations."}, {"t": "p", "html": "FIREFLEYE — aflibercept versus laser in treatment-requiring ROP; use as a trial anchor, not as a universal agent mandate."}]}, {"title": "ADDITIONAL TEXTBOOK / MANUAL REFERENCE", "h1": "", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Indian Academy of Pediatrics (IAP). Standard Treatment Guidelines — STG 144: Retinopathy of Prematurity; STG 123: Early Detection and Early Intervention of a High-Risk Neonate. Current IAP standard-treatment-guideline index verified October 2026."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Indian Academy of Pediatrics / National Neonatology Forum. IAPNNF Quick Reference Guidelines in Neonatology 2026 — relevant Day 11 topics include 'Preterm Brain Injury and Neuroprotection' and 'Posthemorrhagic Hydrocephalus in Preterm Neonates', with supporting oxygen, pain and cord-management statements."}, {"t": "p", "html": "AIIMS WHO Collaborating Centre for Training and Research in Newborn Care. AIIMS Protocols in Neonatology, 2nd ed. (2019) and freely available newbornwhocc.org clinical protocols. AIIMS states that protocols are designed for its or similar contexts and may require adaptation elsewhere."}, {"t": "p", "html": "AIIMS Protocols in Neonatology — Retinopathy of Prematurity protocol: laser/anti-VEGF/surgery options; NICU treatment logistics; cardiorespiratory monitoring; analgesia/sedation and follow-up considerations."}, {"t": "p", "html": "AIIMS WHO-CC Facility-Based Care of Preterm Infant package (2018–19): modules include optimal oxygen administration, developmentally supportive care/pain management, delivery-room stabilization, infection prevention, nutrition and ROP follow-up."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Guideline verification date: 9 October 2026. Reference stack for Day 11: Nelson 22e (primary concept) + Cloherty &amp; Stark 9e (neonatal bedside cross-read) + IAP/IAP–NNF (India-specific paediatric/neonatal guidance) + AIIMS Protocols in Neonatology/AIIMS WHO-CC resources (Indian tertiary-NICU practice) + current named international/national protocols. When guidance differs, the document names the framework rather than blending it."}]}]}], "quizSets": [{"k": 1, "from": 1, "to": 16, "n": 16}, {"k": 2, "from": 17, "to": 32, "n": 16}, {"k": 3, "from": 33, "to": 48, "n": 16}, {"k": 4, "from": 49, "to": 63, "n": 15}, {"k": 5, "from": 64, "to": 78, "n": 15}], "mcqCount": 78};
